THE LAW ITSELF:  Q_2 = q(y_e) a_0^{3/2} / sqrt(G M),  q tabulated for the Route A kernel
========================================================================================================================
     y_e = g_e/a_0 :      0.25     0.50     1.00     1.50     2.00     2.30     3.00     5.00    10.00
     q(y_e)        :   -0.0104  -0.0336  -0.0947  -0.1595  -0.2210  -0.2551  -0.3274  -0.4764  -0.6220
     |q| rises monotonically across this range and only turns over far above it (check k01-3 shows it
     goes to zero as y_e -> infinity).  The Sun sits at y_e = 2.3, near the steepest part of the curve,
     so the prediction is sensitive to the external field but not to any fitted quantity.

========================================================================================================================
THE SAME NUMBER WITH NO Q_2 CONVENTION IN IT: anomalous perihelion precession
========================================================================================================================
     (printed once; the header above is the section title)
     Mars    (canonical): predicted |dvarpi/dt| ~     0.06 mas/century   against a published ephemeris residual bound of order 0.04 mas/century
     Mars    (alt      ): predicted |dvarpi/dt| ~     0.07 mas/century   against a published ephemeris residual bound of order 0.04 mas/century
     Saturn  (canonical): predicted |dvarpi/dt| ~     0.97 mas/century   against a published ephemeris residual bound of order 0.4 mas/century
     Saturn  (alt      ): predicted |dvarpi/dt| ~     1.05 mas/century   against a published ephemeris residual bound of order 0.4 mas/century
     The coefficient here is an orientation-averaged order-of-magnitude (the exact factor depends on the
     angle between the orbit and the Galactic-centre direction and on the eccentricity); it is quoted to
     show that the Q_2 convention is not what drives the verdict.

========================================================================================================================
THE NUMBER, AND THE PUBLISHED BOUND
========================================================================================================================
  Route A exponential kernel:   Q_2 = -2.006e-26 (canonical)   -2.185e-26 (alt)  s^-2
  the same calculation for the simple       kernel: -1.945e-26 (canonical)  -2.106e-26 (alt)
  the same calculation for the standard     kernel: -9.741e-27 (canonical)  -1.265e-26 (alt)
  the same calculation for the sqrt(1+1/y)  kernel: -1.466e-26 (canonical)  -1.671e-26 (alt)
  
  LITERATURE VALUE, NOT COMPUTED HERE, quoted as an order of magnitude only: planetary-ephemeris fits
  bound the MOND external-field quadrupole at |Q_2| of order 3e-27 s^-2 (Milgrom 2009 posed it;
  Blanchet & Novak 2011 computed it for the standard interpolating families; Hees et al. 2014/2016
  derived the ephemeris limit).  Conventions for Q_2 differ between papers by factors of order unity,
  so the ratio below is an order-of-magnitude statement and MUST NOT be quoted as a sigma.
     |Q_2|/bound  =  6.7 (canonical)   7.3 (alt)   for Route A
     |Q_2|/bound  =  6.5 (canonical) for simple
     |Q_2|/bound  =  3.2 (canonical) for standard
     |Q_2|/bound  =  4.9 (canonical) for sqrt(1+1/y)
  
  LambdaCDM / GR + cold dark matter computed beside the framework: Q_2 = 0 EXACTLY, because GR obeys the
  strong equivalence principle -- a uniform external field is removable by going to the free-fall frame.
  There is nothing to fit and nothing to tune; the entire signal is a MOND-only effect.

